1 ANALYZE

   Describes a file's contents as VMS's ANALYZE does, in the same words
   and page layout: an object file, an image, or an RMS file.  The kind
   of file is chosen by the qualifier, which must be given.

   Format

     ANALYZE/OBJECT filespec[,...]
     ANALYZE/IMAGE filespec[,...]
     ANALYZE/RMS_FILE filespec[,...]

   Files are found by MACRO's rules (see MACRO Parameter), and a bare
   name is found beside the file before it.  Errors in a file are
   flagged with "***" where they're found.

2 /IMAGE

   Describes an executable or shareable image: the image header
   (transfer addresses, symbol table locations, the image's name, ident,
   and link time, and each image section descriptor with its flags),
   then the image activator's fixup section (the shareable images the
   image uses, its G^ and .ADDRESS references to them, and the
   protection changes).  Errors are counted at the end.

   Format

     ANALYZE/IMAGE filespec[,...]

3 Parameter

 filespec[,...]

   Image files, of type .EXE unless one is given.

3 Qualifiers
/FIXUP_SECTION

   Shows the fixup section with the header (the default when neither
   /FIXUP_SECTION nor /HEADER is given).

/HEADER

   Shows only the image header.

/HOST

   After a file, makes it a host path, whatever it looks like.

/OUTPUT[=filespec]

   Writes the report to a file instead of the terminal: by default the
   first file's name with the type .ANI, beside it.

3 Examples

   1.$ ANALYZE/IMAGE PROG

     Describes PROG.EXE.

   2.$ ANALYZE/IMAGE/HEADER/OUTPUT DUA1:[000000]PROG

     Writes PROG.EXE's header to DUA1:[000000]PROG.ANI.

2 /OBJECT

   Describes each record of an object file, or of an object library's
   modules: headers, global symbol directory subrecords with their
   flags, each TIR command with the linker's stack depth, STORE
   IMMEDIATE data as hexadecimal and characters, and the end of module
   record.  Errors are counted in a summary at the end.  With any of
   /DBG, /EOM, /GSD, /LNK, /MHD, /TBT, and /TIR, only those kinds of
   records are shown; every record is still checked and counted.

   Format

     ANALYZE/OBJECT filespec[,...]

3 Parameter

 filespec[,...]

   Object files (.OBJ by default), or object libraries (.OLB), whose
   modules are analyzed one after another.

3 Qualifiers
/DBG

   Shows the debugger records.

/EOM

   Shows the end of module records.

/GSD

   Shows the global symbol directory records.

/HOST

   After a file, makes it a host path, whatever it looks like.

/INCLUDE[=(module[,...])]

   Analyzes these modules of an object library; names may hold the
   wildcards * and %.  Without names, or without /INCLUDE, every
   module.

/LNK

   Shows the link option specification records.

/MHD

   Shows the header records, every kind.

/OUTPUT[=filespec]

   Writes the report to a file instead of the terminal: by default the
   first file's name with the type .ANL, beside it.

/TBT

   Shows the traceback records.

/TIR

   Shows the text information and relocation records.

3 Examples

   1.$ ANALYZE/OBJECT "hello.obj"

     Describes a host object file.

   2.$ ANALYZE/OBJECT/GSD/INCLUDE=(MAIN) DUA1:[000000]MINE.OLB

     Shows the global symbol directory of the library's module MAIN.

2 /RMS_FILE

   Describes a file of any organization, reading its blocks directly,
   so that a damaged file can be described.  An indexed file's keys are
   walked, and what's wrong is flagged with "***" lines.

   Format

     ANALYZE/RMS_FILE filespec[,...]

3 Parameter

 filespec[,...]

   The files to describe.  Wildcards are allowed.

3 Qualifiers
/CHECK

   Describes and checks each file's structure (the default).

/FDL

   Writes an FDL file describing the file, for CREATE/FDL or
   CONVERT/FDL: by default the file's name with the type .FDL.  Of a
   list, only the first file's FDL is written.

/HOST

   After a file, makes it a host path, whatever it looks like.

/[NO]OUTPUT[=filespec]

   /OUTPUT writes the report to a file (by default ANALYZE.ANL, or the
   file's name with the type .FDL with /FDL), and shows the count of
   errors, %ANLRMS-I-ERRORS.  /NOOUTPUT shows only the count of errors.
   Without either, the report goes to the terminal.

/STATISTICS

   Adds each key's figures to the report.

/SUMMARY

   Describes the file without walking it.

3 Examples

   1.$ ANALYZE/RMS_FILE PARTS.IDX

     Describes and checks PARTS.IDX.

   2.$ ANALYZE/RMS_FILE/FDL PARTS.IDX

     Writes PARTS.FDL, a description of PARTS.IDX.
